{"abstract":"AI systems that work as intended when first deployed may drift over time, making increasingly inaccurate or biased decisions. Organizations therefore need to ensure the proper ongoing functioning of their AI systems, especially as the world around these AI systems changes. In this article, we describe the strategies used by the Danish Business Authority, an early public-sector adopter of AI, to ensure the effective ongoing evaluation of AI systems and provide four recommendations for other organizations.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver","Nagbøl, Per Rådberg","Müller, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828161"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Paderborn University"],"keyword":["AI","AI governance","Responsible AI"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the danish business authority s approach to the ongoing evaluation of ai systems","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://aisel.aisnet.org/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1628&context=misqe","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828161","ids":["itu-80bd5d83-8460-4c61-9729-4a57033c66fe"],"keyword":["AI","AI Evaluation","AI Governance","Responsible AI"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-AB20DF0A79805B85655713A81993AA1B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://aisel.aisnet.org/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1628&context=misqe","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"paderborn university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Paderborn University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Per Rådberg","key":"nagbølpau","last":"Nagbøl","name":"Nagbøl, Per Rådberg","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"müllerpau","last":"Müller","name":"Müller, Oliver","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["15401960","15401979"],"issn_org":["15401960"],"issue":"2","journal":"MIS Quarterly Executive","pages":"137-150","paperid":"3","publisher":"Indiana University Press","status":"p","title":"MIS Quarterly Executive","title_org":"M I S Quarterly Executive","volume":"24"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-80bd5d83-8460-4c61-9729-4a57033c66fe"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Danish Business Authority’s Approach to the Ongoing Evaluation of AI Systems","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828161","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This paper examines the effect of forced and emergent competition- and cooperation-enhancing mechanisms on joint multisourcing performance. We draw on research on coopetition in IS multisourcing and the literature on the crowding-out effect to theorise the interplay between these mechanisms. We argue that the key to understanding whether these mechanisms complement or substitute each other lies in the distinction between forced and emergent mechanisms, as these respectively invoke either an economic or a social logic among vendors. We test these ideas through a survey study of 108 multisourcing arrangements. Our results show that while a forced competition and an emergent cooperation mechanism can individually improve joint performance in multisourcing, the co-existence of economic and social logics results in a substitutional effect. A complementary effect is achieved when competition and cooperation mechanisms are of the same logic. Our study extends the existing IS outsourcing literature by shedding light on the role of forced and emergent mechanisms, either as competition or cooperation-enhancing, in enhancing multisourcing performance.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Lioliou, Eleni","Krancher, Oliver","Oshri, Ilan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-789558"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["New Zealand","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Queen Mary University of London","The University of Auckland"],"keyword":["Competition","Cooperation","Joint performance","Multisourcing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the complementary and substitutional effects of forced and emergent mechanisms in multisourcing","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsis.2024.101869","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Under this license: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"],"id":"S01-000.00-789558","identifier":[{"id":"85206921881","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-77d0dfc5-fe18-407a-91af-7836e2bebf38"],"keyword":["Competition","Cooperation","Emergent mechanisms","Forced mechanisms","Joint performance","Multisourcing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-77A0D764D22651EE2447AED6E731531B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsis.2024.101869","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"queen mary university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Queen Mary University of London","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nz","key":"the university of auckland","loc":["New Zealand","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"The University of Auckland","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Eleni","key":"liolioupau","last":"Lioliou","name":"Lioliou, Eleni","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ilan","key":"oshripau","last":"Oshri","name":"Oshri, Ilan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jsis.2024.101869","issn":["09638687","18731198"],"issn_org":["09638687"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of Strategic Information Systems","pages":"101869","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Journal of Strategic Information Systems","title_org":"The Journal of Strategic Information Systems","volume":"34"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85206921881"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-77d0dfc5-fe18-407a-91af-7836e2bebf38"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The complementary and substitutional effects of forced and emergent mechanisms in multisourcing","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-789558","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This article seeks to provide deeper insights into the concept of team autonomy within the software industry by investigating the combinations of autonomy and control modes that lead to high perceptions of team autonomy. Additionally, it examines the types of autonomy and control that are most effective for navigating complex environments. The study is grounded in the framework of Complex Adaptive Systems (CAS), integrating interdisciplinary research on autonomy and control to develop a research design. Methodologically, the study employs survey data and qualitative comparative analysis (QCA) to address its research questions. The findings identify three distinct configurations of projects that achieve high team autonomy, demonstrating how the road to high team autonomy can be shaped in various ways in relation to the presence of different modes of control. Using the CAS framework to evaluate these configurations, the third configuration emerges as the most aligned with the framework and empirically the most successful. This configuration is characterized by the absence of control for safeguarding purposes, the presence of control for coordination purposes, and the presence of joint decision-making. The article concludes by discussing the fuzzy and contextual nature of autonomy and its inherent relationship with control. It emphasizes the importance of understanding autonomy within its specific context and highlights the value of applying the CAS framework to grasp the complexity of autonomy-control dynamics. This study contributes to the literature by offering a nuanced perspective on autonomy in teams and its role in addressing the challenges of complexity in projects.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bjerre Olesen, Sigrid","Leimbach, Timo","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828143"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Agile development","Autonomy","Control","Project control and monitoring","Project management","Qualitative comparative analysis QCA"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"three faces of autonomy exploring configurations of high autonomy in software project teams","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/112601817/Three_faces_of_autonomy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828143","ids":["itu-2774a004-2c18-4b47-b3bb-e45a74283742"],"keyword":["Agile Development","Autonomy","Complex adaptive systems (CAS)","Control","Project control and monitoring","Project management","Qualitative comparative analysis (QCA)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6F511E9A078437B34D642E5D876EA8CC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/112601817/Three_faces_of_autonomy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sigrid","key":"bjerreolesenpau","last":"Bjerre Olesen","name":"Bjerre Olesen, Sigrid","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Timo","key":"leimbachpau","last":"Leimbach","name":"Leimbach, Timo","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2774a004-2c18-4b47-b3bb-e45a74283742"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Three faces of autonomy: Exploring configurations of high autonomy in software project teams","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828143","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Decision support systems (DSS) integrating artificial intelligence (AI) hold the potential to significantly enhance organizational decision-making performance and speed in areas such as prognostics in machine maintenance. A key issue for organizations aiming to leverage this potential is to select an appropriate AI-based DSS. In this paper, we develop a delegation perspective to identify decision factors and underlying AI system characteristics that affect the selection of AI-based DSS. Utilizing the analytical hierarchy process method, we derive decision weights for these characteristics and apply them to three archetypes of AI-based DSS designed for prognostics. Additionally, we explore how users’ expertise levels impact their preferences for specific AI system characteristics. The results confirm that Performance is the most important decision factor, followed by Effort and Transparency. In line with these results, we find that the archetypes of prognostics systems using Direct Remaining Useful Life estimation and Similarity-based Matching best fit user preferences. Moreover, we find that novices and experts strongly prefer visual over structural explanations, while users with moderate expertise also value structural explanations to develop their skills further.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Heinrich, Kai","Janiesch, Christian","Krancher, Oliver","Stahlmann, Philip","Wanner, Jonas","Zschech, Patrick"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828152"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Otto-von-Guericke University","Paxray GmbH","TU Dortmund University","Technical University of Dresden"],"keyword":["AI","Adoption","Algorithms","Decision making","Decision theory","Deep learning","Delegation","Explainability","Explainable AI XAI","Information processing","Outsourcing","Regression Analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"decision factors for the selection of ai based decision support systems the case of task delegation in prognostics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0328411","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828152","identifier":[{"id":"105011754290","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-588d32e3-dc7d-4332-8bbd-5f135d6161b3"],"keyword":["AI","Adoption","Algorithms","Decision making","Decision theory","Deep learning","Delegation","Explainability","Explainable AI (XAI)","Information processing","Outsourcing","Regression analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-E0546A01A3CDD4FC19DD58A6C7ECBFB2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0328411","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/file?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0328411&type=printable","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"otto von guericke university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Otto-von-Guericke University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"tu dortmund university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"TU Dortmund University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"de","key":"paxray gmbh","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Paxray GmbH","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"de","key":"technical university of dresden","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Dresden","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kai","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","name":"Heinrich, Kai","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"janieschpau","last":"Janiesch","name":"Janiesch, Christian","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Philip","key":"stahlmannpau","last":"Stahlmann","name":"Stahlmann, Philip","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas","key":"wannerpau","last":"Wanner","name":"Wanner, Jonas","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Patrick","key":"zschechpau","last":"Zschech","name":"Zschech, Patrick","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0328411","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"7","journal":"PLOS ONE","pages":"1","paperid":"e0328411","publisher":"Public Library of Science","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","volume":"20"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105011754290"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-588d32e3-dc7d-4332-8bbd-5f135d6161b3"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Decision factors for the selection of AI-based decision support systems—The case of task delegation in prognostics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828152","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
