{"abstract":"Organizations are making substantial efforts to improve their ambidexterity, i.e., their ability to achieve both exploitation goals (e.g., efficiency, predictability) and exploration goals (e.g., adaptiveness, innovation). Although the practice literature suggests that the technology used in software projects can play a vital role in this regard, there is little research on how technological factors influence ambidexterity in software projects. In this study, we investigate the roles of three technological factors: continuous integration, standardization, and microservice architecture. The results of a multi-informant survey of 95 projects in Switzerland and Denmark show that continuous integration and standardization are associated with higher ambidexterity, while the relationship between microservice architecture and ambidexterity is insignificant. Our article goes beyond existing research by showing that continuous integration and standardization can promote not only exploitation but also exploration. We also discuss reasons for the insignificant connection between microservice architecture and ambidexterity.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver","Biangslev, Christina Staun"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-764511"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Ambidexterity","Contextual ambidexterity","Continuous integration","Standardization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Devoteam Denmark"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"wie technologische faktoren ambidextrie in software projekten beeinflussen","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1365/s40702-024-01112-0","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-764511","ids":["itu-afde28d4-5c50-41b1-8bfa-ee8680529b87"],"keyword":["Ambidexterity","Contextual ambidexterity","Continuous integration","Knowledge specificity","Microservices architecture","Standardization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-101D584AEC28A95711F0F35A71EE5452"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1365/s40702-024-01112-0","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106609403/HDM_R1_final_en.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Devoteam Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christina Staun","key":"biangslevpau","last":"Biangslev","name":"Biangslev, Christina Staun","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["itu-afde28d4-5c50-41b1-8bfa-ee8680529b87"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Wie technologische Faktoren Ambidextrie in Software-Projekten beeinflussen","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-764511","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"For over four decades, a rich body of information systems (IS) sourcing research has examined topics concerning sourcing decisions, governing relationships and performance in various contexts (e.g., outsourcing, multisourcing and offshoring). The last few years have witnessed a significant change in some of the key assumptions in this literature. Organizations and their sourcing practices have been undergoing significant shifts in the digital era, involving not only human but also technology agency (e.g., artificial intelligence), malleable digital assets that can be flexibly recombined (e.g., in platform ecosystems and low-code development), and digital technology that attenuates bounded rationality (e.g., generative artificial intelligence). Such fundamental changes call for the re-examination of such past assumptions in the IS sourcing literature. In this editorial, e discuss the implications of these agential, semiotic, infrastructural, combinatorial, and economic shifts for digital sourcing, that is, research and practice of IS sourcing in the digital era.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver","Sabherwal, Rajiv","Oshri, Ilan","Kotlarsky, Julia"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-747242"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["New Zealand","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The University of Auckland","University of Arkansas"],"keyword":["Emerging technologies","IS sourcing","agential shift","combinatorial shift","digital sourcing","economic shift","semiotic shift"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"digital sourcing a discussion of agential semiotic infrastructural combinatorial and economic shifts","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dje","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/02683962241260841","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-747242","identifier":[{"id":"85195278375","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7f1a758a-6078-4ac9-80f5-48869b8b30e2"],"keyword":["IS sourcing","agential shift","combinatorial shift","digital sourcing","economic shift","emerging technologies","semiotic shift"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B4D5F767DA7C9BEBD7C65578CE8887A6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/02683962241260841","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/reader/10.1177/02683962241260841","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of arkansas","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Arkansas","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nz","key":"the university of auckland","loc":["New Zealand","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"The University of Auckland","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rajiv","key":"sabherwalpau","last":"Sabherwal","name":"Sabherwal, Rajiv","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ilan","key":"oshripau","last":"Oshri","name":"Oshri, Ilan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Julia","key":"kotlarskypau","last":"Kotlarsky","name":"Kotlarsky, Julia","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/02683962241260841","issn":["02683962","14664437"],"issn_org":["02683962"],"issue":"2","journal":"Journal of Information Technology","pages":"262-269","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","status":"p","title":"Journal of Information Technology","volume":"39"},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-85195278375"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7f1a758a-6078-4ac9-80f5-48869b8b30e2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Digital sourcing: A discussion of agential, semiotic, infrastructural, combinatorial, and economic shifts","type":"Editorial","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-747242","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Although there is a large amount of research on adoption and benefits management, we know surprisingly little about how and why managers manage adoption of enterprise systems. By conducting an interpretive case study, we explore managers’ perceptions of adoption management. Through semi-structured interviews, we uncover that two levels of management expressed surprisingly little action to influence users’ adoption towards desired benefits once the application is live. The data analysis found a mental rationalisation pattern that justified the managers’ (lack of) adoption action. We identify these ostensive and performative structures as a project-centric mindset to highlight how the rigid boundaries of project completion are prioritised, thus challenging holistic adoption management.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Falch, Cecilie Caroline","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776434"],"event":{"acronym":"HICSS","title":"Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences"},"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Management Actions"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"why managers don t manage an interpretive case study of information systems adoption management","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://hdl.handle.net/10125/107187","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776434","ids":["itu-a2e230cf-080e-4904-a7fc-820f89a5b358"],"keyword":["Adoption management","Caes Study","Management Actions","Management Justifications","Project Thinking"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E7745DF086A797D38A52CA78E2215210"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://hdl.handle.net/10125/107187","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/21ed6935-5438-43f8-8892-c7a6a4d32c9a/content","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Cecilie Caroline","key":"falchpau","last":"Falch","locid":"d4dc2c50-a749-4f5b-a3f1-763c1dc405fb","name":"Falch, Cecilie Caroline","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"6687-6696","place":"Honolulu","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 57th Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a2e230cf-080e-4904-a7fc-820f89a5b358"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Why Managers Don't Manage: An Interpretive Case Study of Information Systems Adoption Management","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776434","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Machine Learning (ML) projects encounter significant uncertainty due to the search for potential use cases and the opacity of ML models, which may challenge project efficiency and model effectiveness. Taking an Information Processing (IP) View, we examine how projects can counter these sources of uncertainty with appropriate sources of IP capacity, including iterative development and knowledge overlap between data scientists and domain experts. Survey data from 141 ML project teams shows that iterative development and knowledge overlap in the form of domain experts’ data science knowledge can significantly enhance ML project efficiency. Our interaction analysis shows that iterative development and domain experts’ data science knowledge helps address uncertainty in the business sphere (i.e., requirements uncertainty), while data scientists’ domain knowledge helps address uncertainty in the technical sphere (i.e., inscrutability). We conclude by providing implications for the IS ML literature and practice.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver","Oshri, Ilan","Kotlarsky, Julia"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-764513"],"event":{"acronym":"ICIS","title":"International Conference on Information Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["OECD","Oceania"],"country":["New Zealand"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Auckland Business School"],"keyword":["Development projects","Machine learning","Project success"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"knowledge overlap and iterative development in ml projects an information processing view","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106751941/Krancher_et_al_-_Knowlede_Overlap_and_Iterative_Development_in_ML_Projects.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-764513","ids":["itu-d30c2c90-7d9e-4db6-9b8d-f0de7151bae9"],"keyword":["Development projects","Iterative development","Knowledge overlap","Machine learning","Project success"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-942ADB46C3F5F72B8BD062A8175FC840"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106751941/Krancher_et_al_-_Knowlede_Overlap_and_Iterative_Development_in_ML_Projects.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"nz","key":"university of auckland business school","loc":["New Zealand","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"University of Auckland Business School","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ilan","key":"oshripau","last":"Oshri","name":"Oshri, Ilan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Julia","key":"kotlarskypau","last":"Kotlarsky","name":"Kotlarsky, Julia","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00000033"],"journal":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","status":"p","title":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","title_org":"Proceedings of the International Conference on Information Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d30c2c90-7d9e-4db6-9b8d-f0de7151bae9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Knowledge Overlap and Iterative Development in ML Projects: An Information Processing View","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-764513","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Psychological safety continues to pique the interest of scholars in a variety of disciplines of study. Recent research indicates that psychological safety fosters knowledge sharing and norm clarity and complements agile values. Although software quality remains a concern in the software industry, academics have yet to investigate whether and how psychologically safe teams provide superior results. In this study, we explore how psychological safety influences agile teams' quality-related behaviors aimed at enhancing software quality. To widen the empirical coverage and evaluate the results, we chose a two-phase mixed-methods research design with an exploratory qualitative phase (20 interviews) followed by a quantitative phase (survey study, N = 423). Our findings show that, when psychological safety is established in agile software teams, it induces enablers of a social nature that advance the teams' ability to pursue software quality. For example, admitting mistakes and taking initiatives equally help teams learn and invest their learning in their future decisions related to software quality. Past mistakes become points of reference for avoiding them in the future. Individuals become more willing to take initiatives aimed at enhancing quality practices and mitigating software quality issues. We contribute to our endeavor to understand the circumstances that promote software quality. Psychological safety requires organizations, their management, agile teams, and individuals to maintain and propagate safety principles. Our results also suggest that technological tools and procedures can be utilized alongside social strategies to promote software quality.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Zahedi, Mansooreh","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-751522"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Melbourne"],"keyword":["Agile methods","Human Aspects of Software Engineering","Human and social aspects of software engineering","Mixed methods","Psychological safety","Software quality","Team and project work"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the role of psychological safety in promoting software quality in agile teams","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-024-10512-1","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-751522","identifier":[{"id":"85199504510","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-20eaedb0-e20a-42d8-953e-2b18299e21d2","itu-709c1ce6-86b3-46d0-b09e-3a9f56758723"],"keyword":["Agile Methods","Human Aspects of Software Engineering","Human and social aspects of software engineering","Mixed methods","Psychological safety","Software quality","Team and project work"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-34D5A9AD2014E4AFB53BFD379C677B47"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/757113809/s10664-024-10512-1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-024-10512-1","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centered Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"au","key":"university of melbourne","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"University of Melbourne","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","locid":"ef3cb54e-65d7-4493-9bbe-2ae5cc274b2e","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mansooreh","key":"zahedipau","last":"Zahedi","name":"Zahedi, Mansooreh","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10664-024-10512-1","issn":["13823256","15737616"],"issn_org":["13823256"],"issue":"5","journal":"Empirical Software Engineering","paperid":"119","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Empirical Software Engineering","volume":"29"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85199504510"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-20eaedb0-e20a-42d8-953e-2b18299e21d2","itu-709c1ce6-86b3-46d0-b09e-3a9f56758723"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Role of Psychological Safety in Promoting Software Quality in Agile Teams","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-751522","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"In today's world, organizations rely on cybersecurity end-user compliance as an essential practical parameter. Yet cybersecurity compliance remains a challenge, and failures are commonplace. But why? In addressing this question, we argue that ISP compliance is neither too monolithic nor too granular a construct but needs respecification. We empirically investigate cybersecurity antecedents leading to (i) user protection-centric password management and (ii) system protection-centric update compliance dimensions. The results of our survey of 241 users show differentiating behavioral strands intertwined across different types of compliance, highlighting a unique interplay of attitudes, knowledge, and social factors as antecedents to password and update compliance.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Datta, Pratim","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776376"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Kent State University"],"keyword":["Behavioral economics","Biases"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cybersecurity end user compliance password management versus update compliance","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.im.2024.104060","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776376","identifier":[{"id":"85209145889","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1b02ae24-136a-4304-9135-59a77deef0e4"],"keyword":["Behavioral economics","Biases","Diligence","End-user compliance","IT security knowledge","Social networking activity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0E0AC2CCCF1ACF32CE8D2FBAA0501D56"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/107260902/Datta_and_Krancher_-_cybersecurity_end-user_compliance.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.im.2024.104060","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"kent state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Kent State University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pratim","key":"dattapau","last":"Datta","name":"Datta, Pratim","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.im.2024.104060","issn":["03787206","18727530"],"issn_org":["03787206"],"issue":"8","journal":"Information & Management","pages":"1","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Information & Management","volume":"61"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85209145889"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1b02ae24-136a-4304-9135-59a77deef0e4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Cybersecurity end-user compliance: Password management versus update compliance","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776376","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Context: Psychological safety continues to inspire researchers’ curiosity in various fields of study. It has been shown to enhance teams’ performance, efficiency, and learning, among other corollaries. Researchers are stretching the boundaries of these early findings to identify further effects of psychological safety. Recent work shows that psychological safety promotes knowledge sharing, norm clarity, and complements agile values. Objective: Studies show that psychological safety enhance agile values and practices, and some practitioners went as far as to claim “agile doesn't work without psychological safety.” Yet, researchers have not explored its antecedents. In this study, we sought to understand how psychological safety materializes in agile software development teams. Method: We opted for a two-phase mixed-methods study; an exploratory qualitative phase (18 interviews) followed by a quantitative phase (survey study, N = 365) to broaden the empirical coverage and test phase one's findings. Results: Our findings show that psychological safety is established in agile software teams when individuals, the team, and the leadership adopt and promote strategies conducive to promoting a psychologically safe workplace. While openness and no blame towards team members are the “butter and bread” of psychological safety, collective-decision making within the team and the leadership ownership remain the pillars of a psychologically safe workplace. Conversely, team autonomy, technical practices providing a safety net and slack time were not found to promote psychological safety. Conclusion: To institutionalize psychological safety in agile software teams, individuals, teams, and the leadership should consolidate their effort to adopt no blame, openness, collective decision-making in the team, and assuming the ownership of promoting a psychologically safe workplace.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Zahedi, Mansooreh","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S02-092.71-681214"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Melbourne"],"keyword":["Agile software development","Mixed methods","Psychological safety"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"antecedents of psychological safety in agile software development teams","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2023.107267","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-092.71-681214","identifier":[{"id":"85163406056","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-2da54198-6f6a-4f0c-b5e4-2856572500a8","itu-6046a1a1-81d6-46c2-9171-0fd788d173af"],"keyword":["Agile Software development","Mixed-methods","Psychological Safety"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-C2AEC62FB06A6BE9F8D02DBB93B35E1C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2023.107267","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/535716117/1_s2.0_S0950584923001210_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/local/dki-cgi/ws/cris-link?src=aau&id=aau-2da54198-6f6a-4f0c-b5e4-2856572500a8&ti=Antecedents%20of%20Psychological%20Safety%20in%20Agile%20Software%20Development%20Teams","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950584923001210/pdfft?md5=1eb3a138b0afff7b35fa256f413d24b1&pid=1-s2.0-S0950584923001210-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centered Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"au","key":"university of melbourne","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"University of Melbourne","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","locid":"ef3cb54e-65d7-4493-9bbe-2ae5cc274b2e","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mansooreh","key":"zahedipau","last":"Zahedi","name":"Zahedi, Mansooreh","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.infsof.2023.107267","issn":["09505849","18736025"],"issn_org":["09505849"],"journal":"Information and Software Technology","paperid":"107267","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Information and Software Technology","volume":"162"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85163406056"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-2da54198-6f6a-4f0c-b5e4-2856572500a8","itu-6046a1a1-81d6-46c2-9171-0fd788d173af"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Antecedents of Psychological Safety in Agile Software Development Teams","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-092.71-681214","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Our goal was to clarify the focus of IS research on DR. This study is guided by the following research question: What are the IS community‘s most relevant objectives, activities, and challenges regarding DR? To answer this research question, we engaged a panel of more than 35 experienced IS resear-chers spanning diverse research topics within the IS discipline through a comprehensive Delphi study. Details about the application of this methodology are provided at the end of this paper.The well-established Delphi method allowed us to identify and consolidate relevant objectives, activities, and challenges regarding DR as perceived by the IS community. We believe that this undertaking offers a promising starting point for mobilizing a broader community effort toward amplifying responsibility in our IS research efforts to benefit individuals, organizations, and our digital society. Subsequently, we present the main findings of the Delphi study regarding critical objectives, activities, and pertinent challenges to provide an impulse for beneficial key actions for the IS community.","author_count":38,"contributors":["Beverungen, Daniel","zur Heiden, Philipp","Lehrer, Christiane","Trier, Matthias","Bartelheimer, Christian","Brandt, Tobias","Distel, Bettina","Drews, Paul","Ehmke, Jan F.","Fill, Hans-Georg","Flath, Christoph M.","Fridgen, Gilbert","Grisold, Thomas","Janiesch, Christian","Janson, Andreas","Krancher, Oliver","Krönung, Julia","Kundisch, Dennis","Marton, Attila","Mirbabaie, Milad","Morana, Stefan","Mueller, Benjamin","Müller, Oliver","Oberländer, Anna M.","Peters, Christoph","Peukert, Christian","Reuter-Oppermann, Melanie","Riehle, Dennis","Robra-Bissanz, Susanne","Röglinger, Maximilian","Rosenthal, Kristina","Schryen, Guido","Schütte, Reinhard","Strahringer, Susanne","Urbach, Nils","Wessel, Lauri Kristian","Zavolokina, Liudmila","Zschech, Patrick"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-694604"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count