me":"Wessel, Lauri Kristian","rank":36,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"27","corresponding":null,"first":"Liudmila","key":"zavolokinapau","last":"Zavolokina","name":"Zavolokina, Liudmila","rank":37,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"28","corresponding":null,"first":"Patrick","key":"zschechpau","last":"Zschech","name":"Zschech, Patrick","rank":38,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"20","place":"Paderborn","publisher":"Paderborn University","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-7f2624cc-ed7a-4157-aa01-606f128304a9"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Implementing Digital Responsibility through Information Systems Research : A Delphi Study of Objectives, Activities, and Challenges in IS Research","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-694604","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"While multisourcing offers benefits such as access to best-of-breed resources and enhanced competition, it also presents clients with a new governance challenge, namely the need to ensure that vendors not only deliver their individual contributions but also collaborate to produce a coherent joint outcome. Clients may address this challenge by combining bilateral governance focused on each vendor’s individual performance with collective governance aimed at the vendors’ joint performance. However, it is unclear how the simultaneous application of bilateral and collective governance affects multisourcing performance. Indeed, the literature falls short in systematically differentiating these governance mechanisms and empirically examining their interplay. Drawing on existing work on multisourcing and on the outsourcing governance literature, we argue that bilateral and collective governance direct efforts towards different performance dimensions (individual vs. joint), invoke different metaphors (market vs. team), and promote conflicting norms (competitive vs. cooperative), which can result in trade-offs when bilateral and collective governance mechanisms are combined. Results from a survey of 189 multisourcing arrangements support our expectation that bilateral and collective governance promote different performance dimensions. Notably, one collective governance mechanism, conflict management procedures, contributes to both individual and joint performance. We find substitutional effects between bilateral and collective governance in relation to joint performance but not individual performance, indicating that the benefits of collective governance for joint performance are more easily compromised than the benefits of bilateral governance for individual performance. We also observe complementary effects within collective governance mechanisms. Our key contribution lies in theorizing and empirically examining the effects and interplay of bilateral and collective governance in multisourcing.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver","Oshri, Ilan","Kotlarsky, Julia","Dibbern, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384354"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["New Zealand","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The University of Auckland","University of Bern"],"keyword":["Bilateral governance","Collective governance","Competition","Conflict management procedures","Cooperation","Formal governance","Joint performance","Multisourcing","Outcome control"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bilateral collective or both formal governance and performance in multisourcing","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/87094365/Krancher_et_al_Bilateral_collective_or_both_Accepted_Manuscript.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384354","identifier":[{"id":"85131080254","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d1fe77f9-ac95-4904-84bc-1eebdabc9a62"],"keyword":["Bilateral governance","Collective governance","Competition","Conflict management procedures","Cooperation","Formal governance","Multisourcing","Outcome control","joint performance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-9DA407168FCBC5173994B88C64D20F36"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/87094365/Krancher_et_al_Bilateral_collective_or_both_Accepted_Manuscript.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"nz","key":"the university of auckland","loc":["New Zealand","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"The University of Auckland","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"ch","key":"university of bern","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bern","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ilan","key":"oshripau","last":"Oshri","name":"Oshri, Ilan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Julia","key":"kotlarskypau","last":"Kotlarsky","name":"Kotlarsky, Julia","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"dibbernpau","last":"Dibbern","name":"Dibbern, Jens","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["15583457"],"journal":"Journal of the Association for Information Systems","pages":"1211-1234","publisher":"Association for Information Systems","status":"p","title":"Journal of the Association for Information Systems","title_org":"Journal of the Association for Information Systems (JAIS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d1fe77f9-ac95-4904-84bc-1eebdabc9a62"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Bilateral, collective, or both? Formal governance and performance in multisourcing","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384354","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Context: Technical excellence is a nebulous term in agile software development. This vagueness is risky because it may lead to misunderstandings and to agile implementations that may overlook a key principle of agile development. Objective: This study investigates how agile practitioners interpret the concept of technical excellence brought up in Principle 9 of the Agile manifesto. Moreover, we investigate how agile practitioners put the concept into practice and what conditions facilitate putting technical excellence into practice. Methods: We conducted semi-structured interviews with twenty agile practitioners, coded the data inductively, and performed two sessions to validate the emerging findings. Results: We find that technical excellence is first and foremost a mindset that is underpinned by continuous attention to sustainable code, continuous learning, and teamwork. Fostering technical excellence requires the adoption of design and development practices, such as continuous architecting, and is supported by continuous learning. We also identify three enabling conditions for technical excellence: Leadership support, customer buy-in, and psychological safety. These enablers provide teams with leeway to nurture their pursuit of technical excellence. Conclusion: Our findings highlight the key role of people-based strategies in promoting technical excellence in agile software development. They show that the attainment of technical excellence does not only involve technical practices. On the contrary, it relies on social and organizational support and, most importantly, a mindset.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Krancher, Oliver","Paasivaara, Maria"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-006667"],"filters":{"added":"2022-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations","Lappeenranta-Lahti University of Technology"],"keyword":["Agile principles","Agile software development","Scrum","Software Development Methods","Software development","Technical excellence"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the journey to technical excellence in agile software development","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2022.106959","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-006667","identifier":[{"id":"85131396715","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-89ab613f-ffbc-4546-b7d7-11c1873cb2c7","itu-b6c4526e-26a7-47e4-93b2-b16544ae00fe"],"keyword":["Agile Principles","Agile Software development","Scrum","Software Development","Technical Excellence","software development methods"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-23D77839078275AD2948122D743A69CA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950584922001021/pdfft?md5=09d466197abbc460ce3f2393935e15b9&pid=1-s2.0-S0950584922001021-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2022.106959","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/474993822/1_s2.0_S0950584922001021_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101336785/1-s2.0-S0950584922001021-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centered Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fi","key":"lappeenranta lahti university of technology","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Lappeenranta-Lahti University of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","locid":"ef3cb54e-65d7-4493-9bbe-2ae5cc274b2e","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Maria","key":"paasivaarapau","last":"Paasivaara","name":"Paasivaara, Maria","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.infsof.2022.106959","issn":["09505849","18736025"],"issn_org":["09505849"],"issue":"106959","journal":"Information and Software Technology","paperid":"106959","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Information and Software Technology","volume":"150"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85131396715"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-89ab613f-ffbc-4546-b7d7-11c1873cb2c7","itu-b6c4526e-26a7-47e4-93b2-b16544ae00fe"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Journey to Technical Excellence in Agile Software Development","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-006667","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Scrum remains the most popular agile software development method implementation for a variety of reasons; one important motive is to improve software quality. Yet many organizations fail to achieve quality improvements through the use of Scrum, and existing research sheds little light on the value-add of Scrum for software quality. More specifically, (1) how notions of software quality among Scrum practitioners relate to established quality perspectives, (2) how Scrum helps teams to achieve higher software quality and (3) why some teams fail to meet the objective of higher quality. We addressed these gaps through a two-phased qualitative study based on 39 interviews and two in-depth case studies. We find that Scrum practitioners emphasize established notions of external quality comprising of conformity to business needs and absence of defects, while they also value internal quality, especially sustainable software design. Our results show that Scrum helps teams achieve both dimensions of quality by promoting some social antecedents (collaboration, psychological safety, accountability, transparency) and process-induced advantages (iterative development, formal inspection, and adaptation). Our findings unveil how these factors contribute to achieving software quality and under what conditions their effects can fail to materialize. These conditions include inconsistent Scrum implementations, cultural constraints, team tensions, and inaccessibility of end-users. In addition, the complexity of the project aggravates the impact of these conditions. Taken together, these findings show that Scrum can complement established quality assurance and software engineering practices by promoting a social environment that is conducive to creating high-quality software. Based on our findings, we provide specific recommendations for how practitioners can create such an environment.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S02-093.49-001450"],"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Agile","Agile development","Agile methods","Case studies","Case study","Qualitative analysis","Scrum","Software quality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how scrum adds value to achieving software quality","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-022-10208-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-093.49-001450","identifier":[{"id":"36159897","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85138436947","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-1c8a2e23-69fa-46b1-a71c-2f5535de158c","itu-07231397-bf6d-4bf7-8c0d-54d018f2ecec"],"keyword":["Agile","Agile Development","Agile methods","Case Study","Case studies","Qualitative Analysis","SCRUM","software quality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-FF8A4848EF976604FCBC7195EC29314E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/490084865/EMSE_2022_FINAL.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-022-10208-4","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/87094402/EMSE_2022_Preprint.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centered Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","locid":"ef3cb54e-65d7-4493-9bbe-2ae5cc274b2e","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36159897"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10664-022-10208-4","issn":["13823256","15737616"],"issn_org":["13823256"],"issue":"7","journal":"Empirical Software Engineering","paperid":"165","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Empirical Software Engineering","title_org":"Journal of Empirical Software Engineering","volume":"27"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85138436947"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-1c8a2e23-69fa-46b1-a71c-2f5535de158c","itu-07231397-bf6d-4bf7-8c0d-54d018f2ecec"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Scrum Adds Value to Achieving Software Quality?","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-093.49-001450","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The Information Technology (IT) project literature has extensively studied cost overrun. But there is surprisingly little work on cost underrun. In this paper, we explore twelve IT projects that achieved cost underrun, using interviews and documents analysis. Our findings show that projects achieve cost underruns when they actively implement practices to enhance process efficiency. These practices are: Implementing a collaborative environment, budgetary control, capitalizing on previous knowledge, continuous learning during the project, and accommodating uncertainty. Projects also encounter conditions that contribute directly to cost underrun, like scope reduction and savings in spending. Our key contribution lies in uncovering, based on a unique sample, how a set of specific practices can lead to cost underrun.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Østergaard Madsen, Christian","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381333"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Budgetary control","Collaborative environment","Cost underrun","Information Technology projects","Process efficiency"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"spotlight on the positives how do information technology projects achieve cost underruns","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86470000/0731.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381333","ids":["itu-051af2e1-966f-4215-bcf5-14e9d190614d"],"keyword":["Budgetary control","Collaborative environment","Cost underrun","Information Technology projects","Process efficiency"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-1AD67760DDF7C1AC58CFD581D26826D5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86470000/0731.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"østergaardmadsenpau","last":"Østergaard Madsen","locid":"74a08deb-18e1-48d0-a86e-ed6675da92ab","name":"Østergaard Madsen, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-5984-561X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9780998133157"],"pages":"7496-7505","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 55th Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-051af2e1-966f-4215-bcf5-14e9d190614d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Spotlight on the Positives : How Do Information Technology Projects Achieve Cost Underruns?","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381333","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Although government information technology (IT) projects are often claimed to suffer from cost and schedule overrun, current valid evidence of the magnitude of the problem and its antecedents is difficult to find. To address this gap, we present a mixed-methods study based on 54 Danish government IT projects completed between 2011 and 2020. Using archival data and measures established by Flyvbjerg and colleagues, we find that cost and schedule overrun is substantially lower than reported in previous studies. Qualitative analysis based on interviews and documents in a purposefully selected sub-sample shows that projects are more likely to be completed within budget and schedule if managers actively adopt four key practices: building one team, accommodating uncertainty, rigorous project management and capitalizing on previous domain knowledge. Our findings also suggest that scope reductions may help projects complete within budget and schedule. We discuss theoretical and practical implications for IT project management.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Østergaard Madsen, Christian","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383829"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cost overrun","Government IT projects","IT project performance","Project management","Schedule overrun"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"government it projects current evidence of cost and schedule overrun and their antecedents","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85128729914","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383829","identifier":[{"id":"85128729914","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-afdfd15a-3049-4d09-8393-5d5319685f5c"],"keyword":["Cost overrun","Government IT projects","IT project performance","Project management","Schedule overrun"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-106369A05B850ED8DF1843A40869B83D"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Danish Institute for IT Program Management","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"østergaardmadsenpau","last":"Østergaard Madsen","locid":"74a08deb-18e1-48d0-a86e-ed6675da92ab","name":"Østergaard Madsen, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-5984-561X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-95354-6_7","isbn":["9783030953539"],"pages":"109-125","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","subtitle":"Lecture Notes in Information Systems and Organisation","title":"Advances in Information Systems Development","volume":"55"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85128729914"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-afdfd15a-3049-4d09-8393-5d5319685f5c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Government IT Projects : Current Evidence of Cost and Schedule Overrun and Their Antecedents","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383829","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Although the existing literature portrays information technology projects as complex, dynamic, socio-technical endeavors, research on cost and schedule performance mostly focuses on single factors in isolation. In this paper, we use a configurational approach to overcome this disconnect. We collected interview and objective data from 30 IT projects and conducted a qualitative comparative analysis to examine how configurations of project conditions relate to cost and schedule adherence. Our analysis shows four configurations associated with budget or schedule adherence: Prestigious feedback-based projects, lucky projects, disciplined projects, and team-based projects. We discuss the implications of these results for information technology project management research and practice.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver","Østergaard Madsen, Christian","Alami, Adam","Petersson, Connie"],"cuid":["S02-083.86-007643"],"event":{"acronym":"ICIS","end":"2022-12-14","place":"København, dk","start":"2022-12-09","title":"International Conference on Information Systems (ICIS) 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Cost overrun","IS projects","IT Project Portfolio Management Decision making Public organizations","IT project","IT projects","Project management","Project success","Public administration","Public procurement","QCA","Schedule overrun","Scheduling","configurational analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"explanations for budget and schedule overrun revisited a configurational perspective on it projects budget and schedule overrun revisited","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/87112421/ICIS_2022_R1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-083.86-007643","ids":["aau-9daa1a3e-6d7f-4d2b-91f9-c92dad96032d","itu-d1b4479b-ca72-4496-8e78-adda3b4b8346"],"keyword":["Budgetting","IS projects","IT Project, Portfolio Management, Decision-making, Public organizations","IT project","IT projects","Project management","Public Administration","Public Procurement","QCA","Schedule overrun","Scheduling","configurational analysis","cost overrun","project success"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-2-B98919D36B5D87125DC6F3368D1DE4A9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/87112421/ICIS_2022_R1.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centered Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"østergaardmadsenpau","last":"Østergaard Madsen","locid":"74a08deb-18e1-48d0-a86e-ed6675da92ab","name":"Østergaard Madsen, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-5984-561X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","locid":"ef3cb54e-65d7-4493-9bbe-2ae5cc274b2e","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Connie","key":"peterssonpau","last":"Petersson","name":"Petersson, Connie","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00000033"],"journal":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","status":"p","title":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","title_org":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems (ICIS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-9daa1a3e-6d7f-4d2b-91f9-c92dad96032d","itu-d1b4479b-ca72-4496-8e78-adda3b4b8346"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Explanations for budget and schedule overrun revisited – a configurational perspective on IT projects : Budget and schedule overrun revisited","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-083.86-007643","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Platform owners must ensure that the ecosystems around their platforms remain as innovative as possible to meet market needs and keep pace with competing platform ecosystems. To this end, platform owners either attract new complementors with innovative complements or foster innovation among existing complementors. This study takes the perspective of complementors to understand the conditions under which access to the platform owner’s resources contributes to the complementor’s innovativeness. Based on survey data from 179 complementors of different software ecosystems, our findings support the supposition that access to the platform owner's valuable technical and commercial capital drives the complementor’s product and process innovativeness. When it comes to access to social capital (e.g., reputation effects, quality signaling), benefits for innovation (limited to production innovation) only accrue under the condition that the platform owner invests in partner-specific information sharing. Our findings contribute to a better understanding of the role of platform design and partnership management practices by the platform owner in shaping complementor innovation.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hurni, Thomas","Huber, Thomas","Krancher, Oliver","Dibbern, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381869"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["France","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","ESSEC Business School","University of Bern"],"keyword":["Governance","Innovation","Platform ecosystems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how access to resources affects complementor innovation in platform ecosystems","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-381869","ids":["itu-284d56d7-dfb4-4840-b9c1-f137ba261c56"],"keyword":["Platform ecosystems","governance","innovation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-1EA2077B0B0F0A3E402CC492A3127EF4"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ch","key":"university of bern","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bern","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"essec business school","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"ESSEC Business School","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"hurnipau","last":"Hurni","name":"Hurni, Thomas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"huberpau","last":"Huber","name":"Huber, Thomas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"dibbernpau","last":"Dibbern","name":"Dibbern, Jens","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-06543-9","edition":"1","eisbn":["9783031065439"],"isbn":["9783031065422"],"issn":["21968705"],"pages":"127-146","place":"Cham, Switzerland","series":"Progress in IS","status":"p","title":"Digitalization Across Organizational Levels"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-284d56d7-dfb4-4840-b9c1-f137ba261c56"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Access to Resources Affects Complementor Innovation in Platform Ecosystems","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381869","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Information Technology (IT) projects experience cost and schedule overruns, and some fail altogether. We investigated 54 completed government IT projects, completed from 2011 – 2020. We present a mixed-method inquiry into Danish government IT projects. We used archival data to examine cost and schedule overruns in these projects, using measures established by Flyvbjerg. To further inform our understanding of the various drivers that influence these projects cost and schedule overrun, we conducted a qualitative study using interviews and documents analysis. Our findings show that projects in our sample experience much lower cost and schedule overruns than those reported in previous studies. Our qualitative analysis show that projects are more likely to be completed within time and schedule when project managers actively adopt a set of practices that help these projects to perform positively. These practices are: Building one team, accommodating uncertainty, rigorous project management and capitalizing previous domain knowledge.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Østergaard Madsen, Christian","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384821"],"event":{"acronym":"ISD","title":"International Conference on Information Systems Development"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cost overrun","Government IT projects","Project management","Project success","Schedule overrun"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"better than you think exploring cost and schedule overruns in government it projects","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86102413/ISD_2021_Pre_print.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-384821","ids":["itu-f1813e65-406d-49df-a633-1c8fe70d9b10"],"keyword":["Cost overrun","Government IT projects","Project Management","Project success","Schedule overrun"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-CED42B5F6894B5DB2DEDE162AAF924C7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86102413/ISD_2021_Pre_print.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"østergaardmadsenpau","last":"Østergaard Madsen","locid":"74a08deb-18e1-48d0-a86e-ed6675da92ab","name":"Østergaard Madsen, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-5984-561X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"publisher":"Association for Information Systems","status":"p","title":"The 29th International Conference on Information Systems Development (ISD2021)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f1813e65-406d-49df-a633-1c8fe70d9b10"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Better than you think? Exploring cost and schedule overruns in government IT projects","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384821","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Notwithstanding its potential benefits, organizational AI use can lead to unintended consequences like opaque decision-making processes or biased decisions. Hence, a key challenge for organizations these days is to implement procedures that can be used to assess and mitigate the risks of organizational AI use. Although public awareness of AI-related risks is growing, the extant literature provides limited guidance to organizations on how to assess and manage AI risks. Against this background, we conducted an Action Design Research project in collaboration with a government agency with a pioneering AI practice to iteratively build, implement, and evaluate the Artificial Intelligence Risk Assessment (AIRA) tool. Besides the theory-ingrained and empirically evaluated AIRA tool, our key contribution is a set of five design principles for instantiating further instances of this class of artifacts. In comparison to existing AI risk assessment tools, our work emphasizes communication between stakeholders of diverse expertise, estimating the expected real-world positive and negative consequences of AI use, and incorporating performance metrics beyond predictive accuracy, including thus assessments of privacy, fairness, and interpretability.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Nagbøl, Per Rådberg","Krancher, Oliver","Müller, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384538"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Paderborn University"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"designing a risk assessment tool for artificial intelligence systems","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86457234/_PREPRINT_Designing_a_Risk_Assessment_Tool_for_Artificial.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384538","identifier":[{"id":"85113486190","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-de4fb60d-97d0-4e82-bfc7-bef93b5c9954"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-97E2332A981B8715A20F6ADDF4B74A37"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86457234/_PREPRINT_Designing_a_Risk_Assessment_Tool_for_Artificial.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Business Innovation, Information Systems and Digital Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"paderborn university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Paderborn University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Per Rådberg","key":"nagbølpau","last":"Nagbøl","name":"Nagbøl, Per Rådberg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"müllerpau","last":"Müller","name":"Müller, Oliver","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-82405-1_32","eisbn":["9783030824051"],"isbn":["9783030824044"],"pages":"328-339","place":"Gewerbestrasse 11, 6330 Cham, Switzerland","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","subtitle":"16th International Conference on Design Science Research in Information Systems and Technology, DESRIST 2021, Kristiansand, Norway, August 4–6, 2021, Proceedings","title":"The Next Wave of Sociotechnical Design"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85113486190"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-de4fb60d-97d0-4e82-bfc7-bef93b5c9954"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Designing a Risk Assessment Tool for Artificial Intelligence Systems","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384538","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Dedicated complementors are devoted, faithful, and willing to invest in their partnership with a platform owner. Since such complementors promise continuous value co-creation, complementor dedication is an essential objective of platform governance. However, as dedicated complementors also increase their vulnerability vis-à-vis the platform owner, platform governance needs to strike a balance between satisfying global ecosystem needs and the local partnership needs. To understand this challenge better, our study develops six hypotheses on how two fundamental governance mechanisms – i.e., rules and the way in which these rules are practised – independently and symbiotically drive complementor dedication. We test these hypotheses using survey data from 181 complementors. Our findings show that complementors become more dedicated to a platform owner, the more adequate they perceive the rules to be. Furthermore, our findings suggest two sensible strategies to actualise the potential of adequate rules. Platform owners should either refrain entirely from practising rules with situational flexibility and benevolence, thereby achieving moderate complementor dedication. Alternatively, they should practice rules with both flexibility and benevolence at the same time, thereby maximising complementor dedication. Our findings contribute to the literature on platform governance and broader governance literature.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hurni, Thomas","Huber, Thomas","Dibbern, Jens","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383002"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["France","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","ESSEC Business School","University of Bern"],"keyword":["Benevolence","Complementor engagement","Flexibility","Platform ecosystems","Platform governance","Three way interaction","complementor dedication","governance practices","rule adequacy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"complementor dedication in platform ecosystems rule adequacy and the moderating role of flexible and benevolent practices","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85183276/EJIS_Complementor_Dedication_AM.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383002","identifier":[{"id":"85087572259","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-759d597e-a716-4287-89c7-61adec41c8e9"],"keyword":["Complementor engagement","Platform ecosystems","benevolence","complementor dedication","flexibility","governance practices","platform governance","rule adequacy","three-way interaction"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5DBFFE6F7594B48E3BE969500F6BD9DD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85183276/EJIS_Complementor_Dedication_AM.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ch","key":"university of bern","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bern","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"essec business school","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"ESSEC Business School","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"hurnipau","last":"Hurni","name":"Hurni, Thomas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"huberpau","last":"Huber","name":"Huber, Thomas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"dibbernpau","last":"Dibbern","name":"Dibbern, Jens","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/0960085x.2020.1779621","issn":["0960085X","14769344"],"issn_org":["0960085X"],"journal":"European Journal of Information Systems","pages":"1","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","status":"p","title":"European Journal of Information Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85087572259"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-759d597e-a716-4287-89c7-61adec41c8e9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Complementor dedication in platform ecosystems: rule adequacy and the moderating role of flexible and benevolent practices","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383002","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Although agile practices are gaining in popularity, there is little evidence showing how particular agile practices, in particular those involving the client, affect the success of outsourced software projects. Data from a matched survey of sponsors and developers in 60 outsourced information systems projects indicate negative effects of continuous analysis and positive effects of joint decision making and continuous integration on project success. Moreover, interaction analyses show that some positive effects are enhanced and negative effects dampened when requirements risk is high. These findings caution against continuous analysis in outsourced projects while they support joint decision making and continuous integration. The findings also empirically substantiate the largely untested assertion that agile practices help cope with changing requirements.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383788"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Agile practices","Agile requirements engineering","Agile software development","Continuous analysis","Continuous integration","Joint decision making","Project success","Requirements risk"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"agile software development practices and success in outsourced projects the moderating role of requirements risk","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-49392-9_4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383788","identifier":[{"id":"85086144105","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-adafe546-ed56-4012-a36a-cad8c27ac21c"],"keyword":["Agile practices","Agile requirements engineering","Agile software development","Continuous analysis","Continuous integration","Joint decision making","Project success","Requirements risk"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5E1BEB700EB96E1D37AD4871A384D582"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-49392-9_4","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85114428/Krancher2020_Chapter_AgileSoftwareDevelopmentPracti.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Viktoria","key":"straypedh","last":"Stray","name":"Stray, Viktoria","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Rashina","key":"hodapedh","last":"Hoda","name":"Hoda, Rashina","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Maria","key":"paasivaarapedh","last":"Paasivaara","name":"Paasivaara, Maria","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Philippe","key":"kruchtenpedh","last":"Kruchten","name":"Kruchten, Philippe","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-49392-9_4","eisbn":["9783030493929"],"isbn":["9783030493912"],"pages":"56-72","place":"Cham","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"XP 2020: Agile Processes in Software Engineering and Extreme Programming"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85086144105"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-adafe546-ed56-4012-a36a-cad8c27ac21c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Agile Software Development Practices and Success in Outsourced Projects: The Moderating Role of Requirements Risk","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383788","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"IT projects form an essential part of the ongoing transition towards increased digitalization in the public sector. However, according to the extant literature, IT projects in both the public and private sector frequently experience cost and schedule overruns, and some fail altogether. In contrast to the private sector, the scientific knowledge on IT projects in the public sector is limited in several ways. To contribute to this knowledge gap, we present the results of an empirical analysis of 48 completed central government IT projects. Our study analyses data on projects completed from 2011 – 2018, which have been submitted to The Danish Council for ICT following established guidelines and a mandated IT project model. Our study offers empirical and methodological contributions. We present up-to-date valid empirical data about cost and schedule overruns in government IT projects at the national level submitted to the Danish Council for ICT for risk assessment. We find that these IT projects, on average, experience much lower cost overruns than those reported in previous studies. Moreover, we find that these projects, on average, do experience schedule overruns, but lower than those reported in previous studies. At the methodological level, we demonstrate how sampling bias can be addressed in an analysis of IT projects through the application of the Heckman correction method. This sampling bias is an acknowledged, but unaddressed issue in previous project management research.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Østergaard Madsen, Christian","Krancher, Oliver","Schmidt, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383674"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cost overrun","Government IT projects","Schedule overrun"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cost and schedule overrun in government it projects an empirical analysis","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383674","ids":["itu-a4c5b9a6-4ca5-4cc7-92ac-86c897ddefae"],"keyword":["Cost overrun","Government IT projects","Schedule overrun"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E48340428CF04F879B5162CF7C3CA5A2"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Research Centre for Government IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"østergaardmadsenpau","last":"Østergaard Madsen","locid":"74a08deb-18e1-48d0-a86e-ed6675da92ab","name":"Østergaard Madsen, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-5984-561X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"e0edf6ca-5a14-41d8-81cc-77fd67f03ee5","name":"Schmidt, Jens","orcid":"0000-0002-2500-0467","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a4c5b9a6-4ca5-4cc7-92ac-86c897ddefae"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Cost and schedule overrun in Government IT projects. : An empirical analysis","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383674","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Knowledge transfer in the transition phase (the phase during which the service is handed over to a new unit) is one of the most critical and least understood aspects of outsourcing and offshoring projects. This chapter synthe-sizes the findings of 35 studies of knowledge transfer in the transition phase and extrapolates these findings to discuss implications in the era of digital business strategy and agility. A key finding from the review is that months or even years of scaffolded practice through learning tasks are often required before engineers have acquired sufficient domain-specific knowledge to take over the service. These findings suggests that, to harness the potentials associated with digital business strategy and agility while doing outsourcing or offshoring, clients may need to rely on long-term, embedded relationships with vendors, on learning tasks and feedback, and on technologies that reduce complexity.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382105"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Agility","Knowledge transfer","Offshoring","Outsourcing","Transition phase","digital business stategy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"knowledge transfer in the transition phase review of the literature and implications for digital business strategy and agility","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85108043888","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382105","identifier":[{"id":"85108043888","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-38f31d93-60af-436d-a97b-c5190bc3cc11"],"keyword":["Knowledge transfer","agility","digital business stategy","offshoring","outsourcing","transition phase"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-A8BECA90048DEF8C37BFC85462F74EE0"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"edition":"1st","isbn":["9781138489370"],"pages":"177","place":"New York, NY","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"The Routledge Companion to Managing Digital Outsourcing"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85108043888"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-38f31d93-60af-436d-a97b-c5190bc3cc11"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Knowledge Transfer in the Transition Phase: Review of the Literature and Implications for Digital Business Strategy and Agility","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382105","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Organizations benefit from malleable IT only if users perceive the affordances that malleable IT provides for their work. However, theoretical explanations and empirical evidence related to affordance perception are scarce. In this paper, we develop a theoretical framework based on Social Cognitive Theory to explain two different types of affordance perceptions: vicarious and autonomous. Data from a survey of 154 users supports the framework. We find that vicarious affordance perceptions depend on social information and on basic knowledge about the malleable IT. In contrast, autonomous affordance perceptions, which rely on uncertain and cognitively complex search activities, depend on self-efficacy gained through prolonged use and on the knowledge acquired through a learning process that starts with system use and observation of other people’s use, followed by vicarious affordance perceptions. The key contribution of our paper lies in developing and testing explanations for affordance perceptions under malleable IT.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Lehrig, Tim","Krancher, Oliver","Dibbern, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382844"],"event":{"acronym":"ICIS","title":"International Conference on Information Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Bern"],"keyword":["Adaptive Featur Use","Affordance Perception","Affordances","Autonomous Affordance Perception","Learning","Malleable IT","Self efficacy","Social Cognitive Theory","Vicarious Affordance Perception"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"affordance perceptions under malleable information technology a social cognitive theory perspective","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84678598/ICIS_2019_Affordance_Perceptions_2nd_submission_final.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382844","ids":["itu-69485abd-00ee-4e4d-9b31-9dfb8d429e2e"],"keyword":["Adaptive Featur Use","Affordance Perception","Affordances","Autonomous Affordance Perception","Learning","Malleable IT","Self-efficacy","Social Cognitive Theory","Vicarious Affordance Perception"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-FB1703D3AAF732EB76AB23CF49631497"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84678598/ICIS_2019_Affordance_Perceptions_2nd_submission_final.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ch","key":"university of bern","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bern","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tim","key":"lehrigpau","last":"Lehrig","name":"Lehrig, Tim","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"dibbernpau","last":"Dibbern","name":"Dibbern, Jens","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9780996683197"],"pages":"1-17","publisher":"Association for Information Systems","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Fortieth International Conference on Information Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85114902178"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-69485abd-00ee-4e4d-9b31-9dfb8d429e2e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Affordance Perceptions under Malleable Information Technology: A Social Cognitive Theory Perspective","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382844","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Social media platforms bear the promise of enhancing interactions in collaborative learning. Yet, relatively little is known about how short-message feeds, a key feature in many social media platforms, affect collaborative learning. We report the results of a field experiment in which we manipulated the social media platform that 53 teams of four students used for a collaborative learning task over three weeks. We find that students provided with short-message feeds achieved significantly better learning outcomes than students without. While learner engagement was similar in both conditions, learner communication was more focused on the task in the condition with short-message feeds. The short-message feed led learners to curtail social communication and uncertainty statements, which mediated the effect of the short-message feeds on learning outcomes. Our study provides new insights into the effect of short-message feeds on collaborative learning and communication.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver","Dibbern, Jens","Meyer, Paul"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381596"],"event":{"acronym":"ICIS","title":"International Conference on Information Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Meyer Werft","University of Bern"],"keyword":["Collaborative learning","Computer supported collaborative learning","Feed","Learning","Social media","microblogs"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"when less is more how short message feeds in social media platforms affect collaborative learning","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84678628/ICIS_2019_Social_Media_Learning_2nd_submission.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381596","ids":["itu-1576ec27-e157-4970-abfb-8fbc072d3d5a"],"keyword":["Learning","collaborative learning","computer-supported collaborative learning","feed","microblogs","social media"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D7F7A7F5F08FDE4007DF1796187CE03C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84678628/ICIS_2019_Social_Media_Learning_2nd_submission.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://aisel.aisnet.org/icis2019/learning_environ/learning_environ/14/","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ch","key":"university of bern","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bern","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"meyer werft","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Meyer Werft","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"dibbernpau","last":"Dibbern","name":"Dibbern, Jens","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Paul","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Paul","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"1-17","publisher":"Association for Information Systems","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Fortieth International Conference on Information Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1576ec27-e157-4970-abfb-8fbc072d3d5a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"When Less is More: How Short-message Feeds in Social Media Platforms Affect Collaborative Learning","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381596","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"This paper examines joint vendor performance in multi-sourcing arrangements. Using an Information Processing View, we argue that managing interdependencies between multiple vendors imposes substantial information processing (IP) requirements on clients. To achieve high joint performance, clients therefore need to possess sufficient IP capacity. We examine how three sources of IP capacity, two internal (i.e., the client’s inter-vendor governance and the client’s architectural knowledge) and one external (i.e., the guardian vendor), work together in realizing joint performance. Our results show that formal governance and architectural knowledge contribute to joint performance. The guardian vendor contributes to joint performance in settings where the client deploys strong governance but lacks architectural knowledge. This suggests that, contrary to common views in the literature, guardian vendors should not be understood as mediators (or single points of contact) who relieve clients from governance efforts. Instead, guardian vendors are more fruitfully understood as architects, who complement the client’s governance efforts by compensating for knowledge gaps. Put simply, client firms should consider using a guardian vendor to compensate for weak architectural knowledge while still maintaining strong formal and informal governance of all vendors.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Oshri, Ilan","Dibbern, Jens","Kotlarsky, Julia","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382527"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["New Zealand","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","The University of Auckland","University of Bern"],"keyword":["Architectural knowledge","Governance","Guardian","Joint performance","information processing view","multi sourcing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an information processing view on joint vendor performance in multi sourcing the role of the guardian","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84585734/Oshri_Dibbern_Kotlarsky_Krancher_JMIS_2019_Postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382527","identifier":[{"id":"85073542534","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5438ebfa-2cbf-45eb-8dd9-97caa08d9cb7"],"keyword":["architectural knowledge","governance","guardian","information processing view","joint performance","multi-sourcing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-4CAFE6DCB64BA2717CE30EC48680A8A8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84585734/Oshri_Dibbern_Kotlarsky_Krancher_JMIS_2019_Postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"nz","key":"the university of auckland","loc":["New Zealand","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"The University of Auckland","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ch","key":"university of bern","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bern","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ilan","key":"oshripau","last":"Oshri","name":"Oshri, Ilan","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"dibbernpau","last":"Dibbern","name":"Dibbern, Jens","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Julia","key":"kotlarskypau","last":"Kotlarsky","name":"Kotlarsky, Julia","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/07421222.2019.1661091","issn":["07421222","1557928X"],"issn_org":["07421222"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of Management Information Systems","pages":"1248-1283","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","status":"p","title":"Journal of Management Information Systems","volume":"36"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85073542534"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5438ebfa-2cbf-45eb-8dd9-97caa08d9cb7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"An Information Processing View on Joint Vendor Performance in Multi-Sourcing: The Role of the Guardian","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382527","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"IT has become a vital part of our daily lives, requiring new skills among today’s leaders, who need to keep up with competitors and meet the needs of employees and customers. This applies to nearly every industry - from clothing manufacturers to logistics companies. However, many leaders find it difficult to keep up with the impacts of digitalization, but the ability to think and act in a digitally innovative way, is absolutely essential for business leaders. For example, most leaders need to be capable of exploiting the potential of collaborating virtually with employees, customers and business partners. If companies are not aware of “new digital opportunities” they will fail to provide their employees with the appropriate digital tools that support their daily tasks. For instance, we see the increase of ‘Shadow IT’: Employees use IT that is not officially approved by the company and that they own privately. This does not happen because the employees want to do something “illegal”, but simply because they feel it is necessary to use a more efficient tool (such as Whatsapp or Dropbox) to execute their tasks. This may have negative consequences for the companies. However, leaders who know how to leverage the use of shadow IT and think innovatively can gain a lot for their company. From September 2017 until January 2019, we tried to inspire the leaders and provide them with an overview of specific methods and tools to exploit digitalization, whilst reducing the risks associated with it.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Richter, Alexander","Nicolajsen, Hanne Westh","Nielsen, Lene","Müller, Oliver","Krancher, Oliver","Stana, Raluca"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383928"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Leadership"],"level":"com","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"innovation for leadership","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85351311/I4L_Report.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383928","ids":["itu-b6646876-8803-4d5d-a538-294407a94d90"],"keyword":["Leadership"],"lang":"English","level":"Commissioned","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6108B67CDCD71D9F7DD9974CC92B81BA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85351311/I4L_Report.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"richterpau","last":"Richter","name":"Richter, Alexander","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hanne Westh","key":"nicolajsenpau","last":"Nicolajsen","locid":"54728da8-274d-40e6-b119-23318d995721","name":"Nicolajsen, Hanne Westh","orcid":"0000-0002-0158-5034","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"1052b289-f510-4c5a-9662-2db48c12ebc2","name":"Nielsen, Lene","orcid":"0000-0003-1768-7147","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"müllerpau","last":"Müller","name":"Müller, Oliver","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Raluca","key":"stanapau","last":"Stana","name":"Stana, Raluca","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"26","publisher":"IT-Universitetet","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b6646876-8803-4d5d-a538-294407a94d90"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Innovation for Leadership","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383928","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Multisourcing has become a common sourcing model in recent outsourcing practice. Yet, the extant and relevant IS literature has so far offered limited insight into how to stipulate both individual (i.e., individual vendor) and joint (the entire vendor network) performance while ensuring governance efficiency. Our study set about addressing this gap by examining how these three dimensions of multisourcing success can be achieved through formal governance. Specifically, we considered bilateral formal control, collective formal control and conflict arbitration (among vendors) as key formal governance elements. Results from a pan-European survey of client firms pursuing multisourcing projects show that bilateral formal control sets the stage to achieve both individual and joint performance, while conflict arbitration strengthens individual performance, and collective formal control strengthens joint performance. Governance efficiency is improved when both collective formal control and conflict arbitration are high. We also found that conflict arbitration strengthens the positive effect of collective formal control on both individual and joint performance. Our findings highlight the importance of governing inter-vendor relationships in multisourcing arrangements as opposed to relying solely on bilateral governance. Our study extends the limited literature on IS multisourcing, and assists managers in considering the strategies they wish to pursue when choosing appropriate governance mechanisms.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver","Oshri, Ilan","Kotlarsky, Julia","Dibbern, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383748"],"event":{"acronym":"ICIS","title":"International Conference on Information Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Bilateral governance","Collective governance","Formal governance","Joint performance","Multisourcing","conflict arbitration","governance efficiency","multisourcing success"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how formal governance affects multisourcing success a multi level perspective","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83537431/ICIS_2018_Multisourcing_final.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383748","identifier":[{"id":"85062506231","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-aad68d5f-078e-485d-a244-bdb81864e58a"],"keyword":["Multisourcing","bilateral governance","collective governance","conflict arbitration","formal governance","governance efficiency","joint performance","multisourcing success"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-16D0EA5CCC203E9C5DB41282C256E935"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83537431/ICIS_2018_Multisourcing_final.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ilan","key":"oshripau","last":"Oshri","name":"Oshri, Ilan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Julia","key":"kotlarskypau","last":"Kotlarsky","name":"Kotlarsky, Julia","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"dibbernpau","last":"Dibbern","name":"Dibbern, Jens","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00000033"],"journal":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","pages":"1-17","status":"p","title":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","title_org":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems (ICIS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-aad68d5f-078e-485d-a244-bdb81864e58a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Formal Governance Affects Multisourcing Success: A Multi-level Perspective","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383748","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Malleable technology bears the promise of allowing users to flexibly change organizational routines. Although the benefits from malleable technology depend on the extent to which users make use of such technology to change organizational routines, we know little about the factors that shape the intensity of routine change. We report the results of a case study in which we analyzed changes of 24 routines under malleable technology over a period of three years. Our results show that actors often perform a series of consecutive changes rather than one discrete change. We build on the concept of momentum to describe the intensity of these changes. Our emergent theory suggests that momentum is affected by the embeddedness of routines, by existing artefacts, by lead actor traits, and by external knowledge. Our study contributes to theory of routine change by developing explanations for variations in momentum of routine change under malleable technology.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Lehrig, Tim","Krancher, Oliver"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382285"],"event":{"acronym":"ICIS","title":"International Conference on Information Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Artefacts","IT enabled Change","Malleable IT","Momentum","Organizational routines"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"change of organizational routines under malleable information technology explaining variations in momentum","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83537461/Change_of_Organizational_Routines_under_Malleable_Information_Technology.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382285","identifier":[{"id":"85062565312","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-449e245e-7a51-48c9-839d-678ba5b8c49b"],"keyword":["Artefacts","IT-enabled Change","Malleable IT","Momentum","Organizational Routines"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-3FD3B3062C7FD2F86E690BC9644D429E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83537461/Change_of_Organizational_Routines_under_Malleable_Information_Technology.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Tim","key":"lehrigpau","last":"Lehrig","name":"Lehrig, Tim","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00000033"],"journal":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","pages":"1-17","status":"p","title":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems","title_org":"Proceedings / International Conference on Information Systems (ICIS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-449e245e-7a51-48c9-839d-678ba5b8c49b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Change of Organizational Routines under Malleable Information Technology: Explaining Variations in Momentum","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382285","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Project teams increasingly rely on computer-mediated communication. In this paper, we propose that communication within these teams benefits from a communication-awareness feature that summarizes communication in one common place. We argue that such a feature pays off specifically during action episodes, when team members engage in taskwork. We conducted two studies of 51 and 35 project teams to examine how the amount of communication during action episodes relates to team performance under low- versus high-communication awareness. In both studies, we technologically designed communication awareness as the availability of a feed, known from social media platforms, that displays all team-internal, computer-mediated communication. The results show that the communication-awareness feature makes communication during action episodes more beneficial, both in term of effectiveness and efficiency. Zooming into the temporal patterns of communication during action episodes further reveals that high-performing teams in the high-communication-awareness condition stand out in terms of early and steady communication. We conclude by discussing implications for current and future research on team communication and awareness support.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Krancher, Oliver","Dibbern, Jens","Meyer, Paul"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384641"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Meyer Werft","University of Bern"],"keyword":["Social media platforms","Team communication","Team performance","awareness systems","communication awareness","team episodes"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how social media enabled communication awareness enhances project team performance","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83434510/Krancher_Dibbern_Meyer_JAIS_How_Social_Media_Enabled_Communication_Awareness_Enhanced_Project_Team_Performance_Author_Version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384641","identifier":[{"id":"85061988272","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e5287e6e-8c71-4091-aa0c-543475141187"],"keyword":["Team communication","awareness systems","communication awareness","social media platforms","team episodes","team performance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-27E89710B02C077112E2DFB159C4C404"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83434510/Krancher_Dibbern_Meyer_JAIS_How_Social_Media_Enabled_Communication_Awareness_Enhanced_Project_Team_Performance_Author_Version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ch","key":"university of bern","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bern","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"meyer werft","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Meyer Werft","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver","key":"krancherpau","last":"Krancher","locid":"f14f4742-c9d8-4a34-b8e2-d713c3a1f437","name":"Krancher, Oliver","orcid":"0000-0003-3876-3604","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"dibbernpau","last":"Dibbern","name":"Dibbern, Jens","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Paul","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Paul","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.17705/1jais.00510","issn":["15583457"],"issn_org":["15369323"],"issue":"9","journal":"Journal of the Association for Information Systems","pages":"813-856","publisher":"Association for Information Systems","status":"p","title":"Journal of the Association for Information Systems","volume":"19"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85061988272"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e5287e6e-8c71-4091-aa0c-543475141187"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Social Media-Enabled Communication Awareness Enhances Project Team Performance","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384641","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
